---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Helmward — verified AI agent execution" description="Helmward runs AI agents on your own hardware and independently verifies their work — nothing is marked done until a check with no model in the loop confirms it.">
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">◇ The agent operating system</div>
    <h1>Your agents report success.<br><span class="grad">Helmward checks.</span></h1>
    <p class="sub">AI agents tell you the work is done. Sometimes it isn't — and the failure looks exactly like success. Helmward runs your agents on your own hardware and independently verifies every result. Nothing is marked done until a check with no model in the loop confirms it against reality.</p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href="#waitlist">Get early access</a>
      <a class="btn" href="#how">See how it works</a>
    </div>
    <div class="trust">Local-first · independently verified · built in public</div>

    <div class="shot">
      <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span class="t">Helmward · Mission Control</span></div>
      <div class="g">
        <div class="cell"><div class="lab"><span class="dot"></span>Control Plane</div><div class="big">LIVE</div></div>
        <div class="cell"><div class="lab">Task</div><div class="big">verifying</div></div>
        <div class="cell"><div class="lab">Probe</div><div class="big">exit 0</div></div>
        <div class="cell"><div class="lab">Result</div><div class="big">confirmed</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="feat" id="how">
    <div class="sectlead">
      <h2>An agent's word is a claim, not proof.</h2>
      <p>Every task runs through the same gate: the agent does the work, then Helmward independently checks the result — running the actual command, hashing the actual file — with no language model anywhere in the checking path. There is nothing to hallucinate, because the check is deterministic code, not another model's opinion.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">✓</div><h3>Independent verification</h3><p>The task isn't done when the agent says so. It's done when a probe with no model in the loop confirms it — checks a file's hash, reads a command's real exit code. The agent cannot mark its own work complete.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">⛒</div><h3>Fails closed</h3><p>If the check can't confirm — empty output, a probe that never ran, a timeout — the result is <em>not verified</em>, never a silent pass. A verifier that green-lights nothing is worse than no verifier at all.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">◍</div><h3>One control plane</h3><p>Every agent, task, and result in a single live dashboard. A durable task queue with retries, so a dropped worker doesn't lose the work.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">◆</div><h3>Cheap models, checked hard</h3><p>Run inexpensive local models for the bulk of the work. Local models fabricate — that's exactly why the gate exists. Verified either way, so you don't have to trust the model, only the check.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">▣</div><h3>Local-first</h3><p>Runs on your hardware. Your data, your models, your control — not the keys handed to someone else's cloud.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">⧉</div><h3>Bring your own agents</h3><p>Claude, local Hermes/qwen, OpenRouter — plug them in as workers on capability-based task claiming. Swap models without rebuilding.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="band" id="why">
    <h2>Why it's real</h2>
    <p>The verification logic isn't a marketing claim — it's a package you can install today: <code>pip install attested</code>. It fails closed, has zero dependencies, and every one of its checks is covered by tests, including the four ways an earlier version of it quietly passed work that never happened. Helmward is that gate wired into a real engine: a durable task queue, capability-based claiming, and a searchable shared wiki. Built and debugged in public — the build log behind this site is written as the system is built, mistakes included.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="roadmap">
    <div class="sectlead">
      <h2>Planned</h2>
      <p>Honest about what isn't built yet. These are on the roadmap, not shipped.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">◈</div><h3>Verified memory</h3><p>A shared wiki agents query on demand exists today. Next: retrieval that confirms a remembered fact is still true before an agent acts on it — precision recall for small local models, which get worse when flooded with context, not better. It also covers what &quot;self-healing&quot; was reaching for: state that has quietly stopped matching reality gets caught when it is read, not after it fails.</p><div class="badge planned">Planned</div></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">⇄</div><h3>Automatic model routing</h3><p>Today you assign which agent handles what. This would route tasks by complexity automatically.</p><div class="badge planned">Planned</div></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">▤</div><h3>Automated build log</h3><p>Turning the verified task history into published documentation, automatically — currently a manual process.</p><div class="badge planned">Planned</div></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="ic">⬡</div><h3>Multi-tenant hosted version</h3><p>A managed cloud offering for teams who don't want to self-host.</p><div class="badge planned">Planned</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wait" id="waitlist">
